.TH VEILA 1 "November 8, 2026" "Veila 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
veila \- secure Wayland screen locker
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B veila
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B veila daemon
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B veila preview
.BI --preview-png= PATH
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Veila
is a native Wayland screen locker built around the secure
.B ext-session-lock-v1
protocol. It ships as a single
.B veila
binary.
.PP
Start the daemon with
.B veila daemon
from a user service or compositor startup command, then use the other
.B veila
commands to request locks, inspect state, validate configuration, view logs, and manage theme selection. Control commands talk to the running daemon and never start it by themselves.
.PP
For every lock the daemon starts a separate secure curtain process from the same binary. That process owns the session-lock surfaces and appears as
.B veila-curtain
in process listings.
.PP
The internal
.B __curtain --lock
mode authenticates through its own isolated PAM helper. It is intended for development;
.B veila lock
remains the normal entry point. Termination signals never authorize an unlock.
.PP
The internal
.B __lock
supervisor is available for daemonless development tests. It owns one lock lifetime;
.B --daemonize --wait-ready
returns after secure startup. Normal lock and service commands continue using the daemon.
A failed curtain is replaced with the emergency prompt
and maximum authentication backoff.
After secure confirmation, recovery retries indefinitely
with delays capped at five seconds.
After secure confirmation the internal supervisor runs logind, sleep/resume,
configured power actions and locked suspend, live widgets and automatic reload.
Opt-in fprintd verification also runs after secure confirmation.
The non-match limit persists across retries, sleep and curtain recovery.
Password authentication remains available if fingerprint verification stops.
.PP
.B veila idle
runs the optional daemonless idle/sleep watcher and starts the internal supervisor
for each lock. Use it instead of the daemon when testing this transition;
the installed service and public lock command still use the daemon.
It watches the same TOML config, themes and includes; SIGHUP forces a reload.
Invalid reloads keep the last valid settings. Stopping the watcher leaves
an acquired lock running. Logind's finite sleep delay can expire before locking.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B idle [--config=PATH] [--session-id=ID]
Watch compositor idle, logind Lock and sleep events. Do not run alongside
the legacy daemon. Does not need to run for direct supervisor locking.
.TP
.B daemon
Start the Veila daemon. See
.BR DAEMON .
.TP
.B preview --preview-png=PATH
Render the lockscreen to a PNG without locking the session. Run
.B veila preview --help
for the preview options such as
.BR --preview-size .
.TP
.B lock
Ask the running daemon to lock the current logind session. Returns when the request is accepted, before compositor lock confirmation.
.TP
.B lock --wait-ready
Wait for compositor lock confirmation and rich startup readiness. If rich readiness times out after confirmation, the secure curtain and authentication remain active. Release or curtain exit before readiness returns an error.
.TP
.B status
Print daemon runtime status.
.TP
.B health
Print build and platform information.
.TP
.B doctor
Run read-only local diagnostics for the Wayland session, PAM service, daemon socket, user services, config, and session-lock protocol support.
.TP
.B check-config
Validate the active configuration and selected theme without starting the daemon or locking the session.
.TP
.B init [--theme NAME] [--force]
Create a starter configuration at the default config path, or replace an existing one when
.B --force
is supplied.
.TP
.B reload
Ask the running daemon to reload configuration from disk.
.TP
.B stop
Stop the running daemon when the session is unlocked. Returns an error while a lock is active.
.TP
.B logs
Show recent logs from the packaged
.B veila.service
user service.
.TP
.B theme list
List bundled theme presets.
.TP
.B theme current
Print the active theme selection and resolved theme source.
.TP
.B theme print NAME
Print the raw source for a bundled or user theme.
.TP
.B theme set NAME
Set the top-level theme key in the active configuration and best-effort reload a running daemon.
.TP
.B theme unset
Remove the top-level theme key from the active configuration and best-effort reload a running daemon.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -h, --help
Show command help.
.TP
.B -v, --version
Print the local Veila version.
.TP
.BI --config= PATH
Use a specific configuration file for commands that read or mutate config.
.TP
.B --force-emergency-ui
Combine with
.B lock
to test the built-in emergency unlock prompt.
.TP
.B --latency-report[=verbose]
Combine with
.B lock --wait-ready
to print startup timing details.
.SH LOGS OPTIONS
.TP
.B --follow
Follow log output.
.TP
.B --file
Read the configured file log instead of the systemd user journal.
.TP
.BI --since= WHEN
Show logs since a journalctl-compatible time or a short duration such as
.B 10m
or
.BR 2h .
.TP
.BI --lines= N
Limit the number of log lines.
.TP
.B --daemon, --curtain, --ui, --all
Select which process logs to show.
.SH DAEMON
.B veila daemon
accepts only startup options. Use the other
.B veila
commands to control a running daemon.
.PP
The former
.B veilad
command name still starts the daemon for one release when it points at the
.B veila
binary, and logs a deprecation warning.
.PP
Daemon options:
.TP
.BI --config= PATH
Load a specific config file.
.TP
.BI --log-file= PATH
Append daemon logs to a file for this launch.
.TP
.BI --session-id= ID
Select a local interactive logind session belonging to the current user.
An invalid or ineligible explicit id fails startup instead of falling back.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The default config file is
.BR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/veila/config.toml ,
falling back to
.BR $HOME/.config/veila/config.toml .
.PP
If no config file exists, Veila uses the bundled default theme and hardcoded safety defaults. Run
.B veila init
to create a starter config explicitly.
.PP
The
.B [idle]
section controls locking that the daemon triggers by itself:
.B enabled
(default false) locks after
.B lock_after_seconds
(default 300, clamped to 5..86400) of compositor-reported inactivity through
.BR ext-idle-notify-v1 ,
and
.B lock_before_sleep
(default true) locks the session before suspend while holding a logind delay inhibitor. Both apply live after
.BR "veila reload" .
.SH FONTS
Bundled fonts provide the initial password UI. After compositor confirmation,
Veila uses Fontconfig's fc-match to select a bounded set of configured and script
fallback faces. Optional fonts load off the Wayland thread and a completed load
redraws the scene without interrupting password entry.
Install fontconfig and fonts covering the scripts you use. Missing, malformed,
oversized or slow optional fonts retain the bundled fallback; characters outside
its coverage can appear as missing glyphs until a suitable face is loaded.
Previews and prewarm use the same font selection rules.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/veila/config.toml
Default user configuration file.
.TP
.I ~/.config/veila/themes/
User theme directory.
.TP
.I /usr/share/veila/
Packaged assets, including bundled themes, fonts, icons, and systemd unit files.
.TP
.I /etc/pam.d/veila
PAM service used for password authentication.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/veila.service
Packaged daemon user service. It runs
.BR "veila daemon" .
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/veilad.service
Compatibility alias for
.B veila.service
so existing enablements keep working. It will be removed in a future release.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VEILA_ASSET_DIR
Overrides the packaged asset directory.
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Controls tracing verbosity for development and troubleshooting.
.SH SECURITY NOTES
Veila uses secure Wayland session-lock surfaces. A compositor must advertise
.B ext_session_lock_manager_v1
for the real lock path to work. Regular fullscreen windows are not treated as a secure lock screen.
.PP
Unlock authority stays with the daemon and logind state. The UI forwards authentication attempts but does not self-authorize unlock.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Start the daemon manually:
.B veila daemon
.TP
Render a preview screenshot:
.B veila preview --preview-png=/tmp/veila.png --preview-size=1920x1080
.TP
Lock and wait for readiness:
.B veila lock --wait-ready
.TP
Validate config:
.B veila check-config
.TP
Create a starter config:
.B veila init --theme default
.TP
Enable packaged user services:
.B systemctl --user enable --now veila.service
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR pam (8)
